So `loop: false` is correct.

Now let me think about the "steady rate" requirement. With linear interpolation between two keys (0 at time 0, 1 at time durationSec), the value changes linearly, which is a steady rate. Good.

"After that it stays at 1 until we set it ourselves" - With `loop: false`, the group stops at the end (note 4: "A non-looping property group that reaches toTime writes the end value, sets isPlaying false and stops writing"). So after the fade completes, `isPlaying` is false and it stops writing. The value stays at 1 (the last written value). Good.

"Several panels may fade at the same time on the same manager, each with its own duration." - Each call creates its own group attached to the same manager. They'll all be updated by `updateAnimationManager`. Good.

One concern: note 11 says "A property group's weight has no effect unless enablePropertyAnimationBlending was called on the manager. Without it each group writes its full value, and on a shared property the later-attached group wins." Since each panel has its own `alpha` property (different objects), there's no conflict. Good.

