```

This looks correct. Let me double-check against all the requirements:

1. ✅ Starts at once on `manager` - `createPropertyAnimationGroup` attaches and starts playing (note 2).
2. ✅ Takes `panel.alpha` from 0 to 1 at a steady rate - linear interpolation between keys at 0 and 1.
3. ✅ Over `durationMs` milliseconds - keys at time 0 and durationMs/1000 seconds.
4. ✅ After that stays at 1 - non-looping group stops at end, writes end value (note 4).
5. ✅ Multiple panels can fade simultaneously - each creates its own group on the same manager.
6. ✅ Return the group, read `isPlaying` - the group's `isPlaying` becomes false when the fade completes (note 4).

I'm fairly confident this is correct. The only thing I'm slightly unsure about is whether there could be any issue with the clip name colliding if multiple fades are created, but the name is just for identification and shouldn't affect behavior.

Actually, wait - let me reconsider. Note 2 says "createPropertyAnimationGroup attaches the group to the manager and starts it playing, with loopAnimation true unless loop: false is passed. It writes nothing to the target until the next updateAnimationManager."

So the first write happens on the next `updateAnimationManager` call. That means at the moment `fadeIn` returns, `panel.alpha` hasn't been changed yet. But the group is playing. On the next update, it will write the value at its current time (which is 0 initially, so it writes 0). Then as time progresses, it writes increasing values. This is fine - the fade "starts at once" in the sense that the group is playing and will begin writing on the next frame update.

